L('java.types').ex[4]=M('You write `int n;` inside main and then read n. What happens?',['It prints 0','It prints null','The program does not compile','It prints undefined'],2,'A local variable is a slot for this method call. Java will not let you read it until you assign it. Fields on an object, which you meet with classes, do get a default. Locals do not.');
L('java.types').ex[7]=M('What does `var count = 0;` mean?',['count can later hold a String','The compiler fixes count as an int, inferred from 0','count has no type','var is not legal Java'],1,'var (Java 10+) asks the compiler to write the type for you. 0 is an int, so count is an int for the rest of its life. It is not a dynamic or JavaScript variable.');
L('java.types').tip='Java checks types when the file compiles, not when a line happens to run. A primitive (int, double, boolean, char, long) stores the value in the variable itself. String and other objects do not: the variable stores a reference, and the next lesson says what that means. var does not loosen this. It only lets the compiler fill in a local type from the initializer. This course compiles against Java 21.';
L('java.types').ex[0].why='int is one of the eight primitives: the value sits in the variable. Integer is the object wrapper for an int. String is a class. ArrayList is a resizable list you will build later; it is a class too, not a primitive.';

L('java.strings').tip='A String variable holds a reference to an object, which is why == is the wrong question. == asks whether two variables point at the same object. equals asks whether the characters match. Strings are immutable: toUpperCase does not edit the text, it returns a new String, and the old one is unchanged if you ignore the result. Immutability is what makes sharing a string safe.';

L('java.control').tip='Conditions are questions with a yes or no, so they must be boolean. Java will not treat 0 or null as false. if, while, do-while, and for are the ways to repeat or choose. A classic switch falls through into the next case unless you stop it. An arrow switch (Java 14+, normal in 21) is an expression: it yields a value and does not fall through. The enhanced for (for (int n : nums)) walks every element; arrays are the next lesson, and the colon form shows up there.';

L('java.methods').tip='A method packages a job: return type, name, parameters, body. Java is always pass-by-value. The value copied for an object is the reference from the memory lesson, not the object. The method can follow that reference and change the object, but assigning the parameter to a new object does not change the caller\'s variable. A method declared to return int must return an int on every path.';

L('java.arrays').tip='An array is a fixed row of slots on the heap. The variable holds a reference, so int[] b = a copies the reference and both names see the same row. That is the memory lesson, applied. The length is fixed at creation, which is what makes a[i] a single jump. The last legal index is length - 1. Walking off the end throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.';

L('java.interfaces').ex[4].why='One abstract method means the interface is a single job, such as "compare these" or "run this". A later lesson writes that job as a lambda. You only need the idea of a single-method contract here.';
L('java.interfaces').ex[5]=M('Why depend on an interface instead of a concrete class?',['It runs faster','Callers depend on the contract, so you can swap the class later','Java requires it','It uses less memory'],1,'Callers should need "what it can do", not "which class happens to do it". You can replace the class without rewriting them.');

L('java.maps').ex[3]=F('Add 1 to the count for word w, using 0 when w is missing.','counts.put(w, counts.___(w, 0) + 1);',['getOrDefault','get','merge','compute'],0,'getOrDefault returns the stored count, or 0 when w is absent. You add 1 and put the result back. merge and method references are the lambda lesson; you do not need them for this.');

L('java.refs').tip='This is the second pass on memory. You already know a primitive copies a value and an object variable holds a reference. The traps here are aliasing (two names, one object), the Integer cache making == look like equality for some numbers, null, and the garbage collector, which reclaims an object only when no live reference can reach it. Optional, a generic "maybe" type, waits until generics are familiar. null is the one you will meet first.';
L('java.refs').ex[7]=O('String s = "hi";\nString t = new String("hi");\nSystem.out.println((s == t) + " " + s.equals(t));',['true true','false true','false false','true false'],1,'new String("hi") is a different object with the same characters. == compares references and is false. equals compares the characters and is true.');

L('java.records').tip=L('java.records').tip+' Pattern matching (instanceof String s) is the Modern Java lesson: the check and the name happen together.';

L('fx.life').tip='JavaFX is not bundled with the JDK. Since Java 11 it has been the separate OpenJFX project. This course targets OpenJFX 21 or newer, which you add as a dependency or as modules. '+L('fx.life').tip;
L('fx.events').tip=L('fx.events').tip+' The form e -> is a lambda: a function value you hand to the button. The Java lambdas lesson is the full story. Here, read it as "when clicked, run this."';

L('css.box').tip='The browser lays out boxes, not tags. Every element becomes a box, and the box model is the contract for how big that box is: content, then padding, then border, then margin outside. box-sizing: border-box makes the width you wrote include padding and border, which is what you usually meant. Specificity (inline, then #id, then class, then element) is how the cascade picks a winner when two rules disagree.';

const SHEETS={
'java.types':['The compiler rejects a type mismatch before the program runs. That is the point of a static type.','A primitive stores the value in the variable. Assigning copies the value.','var infers a local type from the initializer. The type is still fixed.','A local variable must be assigned before it is read. It does not get a silent default.','Widening (int to double) is safe. Narrowing (double to int) drops information and needs a cast.'],
'java.strings':['A String variable holds a reference. == compares references. equals compares characters.','Methods return a new String. If you ignore the result, the original text is unchanged.','Put the literal first ("yes".equals(input)) so a null input does not throw.','A char is one 16-bit unit, not "a character" in every language. The Swift track comes back to this.'],
'java.control':['A condition is a boolean. Numbers and nulls are not truthy in Java.','break leaves the loop. continue skips to the next pass.','A classic switch falls through. An arrow switch yields a value and does not fall through.','A do-while body runs once before the condition is asked.'],
'java.methods':['Pass-by-value copies the primitive, or copies the reference. It does not copy the object.','Assigning the parameter inside the method does not retarget the caller\'s variable.','Every path in a non-void method must return a value of the declared type.','Overloading is two methods with the same name and different parameters. The compiler picks.'],
'java.arrays':['b = a copies the reference. Both variables see one array. clone or Arrays.copyOf makes a second row.','length is a field. The last index is length - 1.','A new int array is filled with 0. A new object array is filled with null.','A two-dimensional array is an array of arrays, so rows can have different lengths.'],
'java.classes':['A class is the blueprint. new builds an object and runs a constructor.','this is the object the method was called on. this.name reaches the field when a parameter hides it.','private fields plus methods are how the object protects its own data.','A constructor\'s job is to leave the object in a usable state.'],
'java.inherit':['extends means is-a. The subclass is a kind of the superclass, not a container for it.','The superclass constructor runs first, even when you do not write super().','Overriding replaces a method for the real object. The variable\'s type does not pick the method.','@Override asks the compiler to prove you actually overrode something.'],
'java.interfaces':['implements means "I keep this contract." extends is for classes.','An abstract class can hold fields and half-finished code. An interface is the contract.','A class can implement many interfaces and extend one class.','Prefer the contract in method signatures so callers are not glued to one class.'],
'java.refs':['Two variables can point at one object. Changing the object is visible through both names.','== on Integer is not value equality. The cache makes some numbers share an object and others not. Use equals.','null means the reference points nowhere. Calling a method through it throws NullPointerException.','The garbage collector frees objects that no live reference can reach. There is no delete.'],
'java.exceptions':['An exception is an object that interrupts the normal path. catch handles that type.','Checked exceptions must be handled or declared. Unchecked ones are for bugs and bad arguments.','finally runs on the way out, including when you return.','A catch that ignores the exception hides the bug. Handle it, log it, or let it travel.'],
'java.lists':['ArrayList is a growable array of references. Generics say what those references point at.','Type arguments are classes, so List<int> is illegal and List<Integer> is the list of ints.','remove(int) removes by index. An immutable list from List.of refuses add.','Do not add or remove inside a for-each. The iterator notices and throws.'],
'java.maps':['A map answers "what is stored under this key?" A set answers "is this already here?"','put replaces the value for a key. get on a missing key returns null, and unboxing that null throws.','getOrDefault is the null-safe read. TreeMap keeps keys sorted. HashMap does not.','A set ignores a second add of an equal element, so its size does not grow.'],
'java.lambdas':['A lambda is an object that implements a one-method interface, written as the method body.','A stream is a recipe. filter and map do nothing until a terminal step such as count or toList pulls values.','The lambda can read a local variable only when that variable is not reassigned.','Method references (Integer::sum) are lambdas that call an existing method.'],
'java.records':['A record is a transparent, immutable carrier. The compiler writes the constructor, accessors, equals, hashCode, and toString.','equals compares the components. == still asks whether it is the same object.','An enum is a closed set of named constants. == is safe because each constant is one object.','instanceof String s tests the type and binds s. That form is pattern matching.'],
'java.sort':['Comparable is the type\'s own order. A Comparator is an order you pass in for this call.','List.sort needs that comparator. There is no zero-argument sort on a list.','Comparator.comparing picks a key, thenComparing breaks ties.','Sorting a list changes that list. A stream sorted() leaves the source alone and returns a new result.'],
'java.recursion':['Each call gets its own stack frame and its own parameters. That is the stack from the memory lesson.','A base case returns without calling again. Every other call must move toward it.','If the argument does not shrink, the frames pile up until the stack overflows.','Recursion is a loop written as a function. Use it when the data is nested, and know the depth.'],
'java.bigo':['Big-O says how the work grows with n, not how many milliseconds a small example took.','O(1) does not grow. O(log n) halves. O(n) walks the input. O(n log n) is a good comparison sort. O(n²) is nested work on n.','Constants are dropped. Two loops in a row are still linear. A loop inside a loop is not.','Binary search needs sorted data. On unsorted data the result is not an answer.'],
'java.threads':['A thread is another path through your methods. start begins that path. run just calls the method on this thread.','join waits until the other thread finishes.','Two threads writing the same variable can lose updates. synchronized or a concurrent class is the lock.','The UI toolkits (JavaFX especially) allow scene-graph calls on one thread only.'],
'java.solid':['Single responsibility: one reason to change. A class that parses and also saves and also draws will change for three bosses.','Open/closed: add a new case by adding a type, not by editing a growing if chain.','Liskov: a subclass must be usable anywhere the superclass was promised.','Depend on the interface so the caller does not construct the concrete class itself.'],
'java.files':['Path is a name. Files is the class that reads and writes. The path does not open the file by existing.','Most of these methods throw the checked IOException. The caller has to have a plan.','try-with-resources closes the reader when the block ends, including when it ends by throwing.','readString is the small-file tool. A stream of lines is the big-file tool.'],
'java.junit':['A test is a small program that fails when the behavior changes. That is how you notice a refactor broke something.','assertEquals takes the expected value first, then the actual value, so the failure sentence reads correctly.','@Test marks the method. assertThrows checks that the bug-shaped input really throws.','Arrange, act, assert: set up the values, call the method, then check one outcome.'],
'java.patterns':['Most interview problems are a known shape: two pointers, a hash map, a sliding window, a stack.','Name the shape before you write code. The shape tells you the complexity.','A HashMap turns "have I seen the complement?" into an expected O(1) lookup.','Practice the shape on a tiny input you can simulate by hand, then code it.'],
'fx.life':['OpenJFX 21+ is a library you add. It is not in the JDK.','Stage is the window, Scene is what the window shows, nodes form a tree.','launch calls init, then start on the JavaFX thread, then stop when the app exits.','The window stays hidden until you call stage.show().'],
'fx.layout':['A pane is a parent that positions its children. VBox stacks vertically, HBox horizontally, BorderPane by region, GridPane by row and column.','A node has one parent. Adding the same label twice throws.','Controls (Button, Label, TextField) are nodes that accept input or show a value.','Spacing and padding are properties of the pane, not of the window.'],
'fx.events':['setOnAction registers a lambda that runs when the button fires an ActionEvent.','A property is a value that can be watched. bind makes one property follow another.','FXML is the node tree written as XML. fx:id connects a tag to an @FXML field.','A bound property refuses set until you unbind it. The binding is the source of truth.'],
'fx.css':['JavaFX speaks CSS with -fx- properties. background-color does not color a button. -fx-background-color does.','A stylesheet is attached to the scene. A style class is attached to a node.','The selector .button matches the style class, not a Java method.','Inline setStyle beats the stylesheet, which is why a later class change can look like it "did nothing."'],
'fx.tasks':['The JavaFX thread draws and delivers events. Long work on that thread freezes the window.','A Task runs the long work off the thread. Platform.runLater posts the result back.','Touching a label from a plain new Thread is a race against the scene graph.','succeed and the message property are the Task\'s way to publish progress safely.'],
'css.html':['The tag is a claim about meaning. A button is a button so the keyboard and the screen reader already know what to do.','One main landmark, a real heading order, and a label for every input are the skeleton of an accessible page.','A div with a click handler has no role and no keyboard behavior unless you rebuild them.','alt is the text alternative for an image, not a tooltip.'],
'css.box':['The used width is content, padding, and border, and margin sits outside that.','border-box puts padding and border inside the width you wrote. content-box adds them on.','Margin collapses between some blocks. Padding never collapses.','Specificity is the tie-break. It is not a measure of "importance" you should fight with deeper selectors.'],
'css.flex':['Flexbox is one direction: a row or a column. Grid is both.','justify-content moves items along the main axis. align-items moves them on the cross axis.','flex: 1 shares free space. width: 100% on every child asks for more than the row has.','gap is the space between items, and it does not add a margin on the outer edge.'],
'css.responsive':['Start from the narrow screen and add min-width queries for larger ones. The base style is the phone.','The viewport meta tag is what makes those widths mean the device, not a desktop page shrunk down.','A length in a query needs a unit. 600 is not 600px.','rem follows the user\'s font size. px does not.'],
'css.vars':['A custom property is a value you name, so a theme change is one assignment.','var(--name) reads it. The dashes are part of the name.','A property inherits down the tree, which is why setting it on a parent restyles the children.','Animate transform and opacity when you can. They avoid rebuilding layout.'],
'swift.basics':['let is a constant binding. var can be assigned again. The type is still fixed.','Swift infers the type from the value. 42 is an Int. 0.0 is a Double.','An Optional is a value that might be missing. nil is the missing case, not a null reference you can message.','if let, guard let, and ?? unwrap it. A trailing ! crashes when the value is missing.'],
'swift.values':['Assigning a struct or enum copies the value. Assigning a class copies the reference.','Two struct variables do not see each other\'s later changes. Two class variables do.','SwiftUI views are structs on purpose: cheap to copy, and the framework redraws from the new value.','Use a class when many views must share one identity, which the navigation lesson does for models.'],
'swift.errors':['throws marks a function that can fail with a value. try marks the call.','do/catch handles the error. try? turns failure into nil. try! crashes on failure.','A SwiftUI View is a struct with a body. @State is state the view owns, and writing it asks for a new body.','@Binding is a reference to state someone else owns. The child writes, the parent stores.'],
'swift.protocols':['A protocol is a list of requirements. A type conforms with a colon, not with extends.','An extension adds behavior. It can provide a default so conformers are not empty.','A closure is a function value. { a, b in a + b } is the inline form.','Array, Dictionary, and Set are structs. Passing one copies it (with copy-on-write for the storage).'],
'swift.enums':['A case is one of a closed set. switch must cover every case, or use default.','Associated values let a case carry data. Raw values are a fixed code for each case.','Case names are case-sensitive. .red and .Red are not the same case.','A mutating method on a struct is allowed to replace self, because the struct is a value.']
};
for(const id of Object.keys(SHEETS)){const l=L(id);if(l&&!l.sheet)l.sheet=SHEETS[id]}

const run={id:'java.run',name:'How a Program Runs',
tip:'A .java file is text. javac compiles it to a .class file of bytecode, which is not machine code for your CPU. The java command starts a Java Virtual Machine, and that JVM loads the bytecode, checks it, and interprets it or JIT-compiles the hot parts to native code. The JVM is why one class file can run on another operating system. This course targets Java 21, the long-term-support release most jobs run today. Java 25 is the next LTS and adds more on top; the model here is the same one.',
sheet:['Source is .java. Bytecode is .class. The JVM runs the bytecode.','javac compiles. java starts the JVM and calls main.','The JIT turns hot bytecode into native code for the CPU you actually have.','Java 21 is the LTS this course uses. Java 25 is the newer LTS.','A program starts at main. A method does not run until something calls it.'],
ex:[
M('What does javac write?',['Machine code for your CPU','A .class file of bytecode','An HTML page','A .java file with types removed'],1,'Bytecode is the JVM\'s instruction set. The JVM, not the class file, knows how your CPU works.'),
M('Why can the same .class file run on Linux and on Windows?',['The file contains both operating systems','A JVM on each machine turns the bytecode into what that machine can run','javac secretly recompiles it','Class files are zip archives of the source'],1,'You ship bytecode. Each computer supplies a JVM that loads it and runs it there.'),
M('Which release is the baseline for this course?',['Java 8 only, because it is the oldest LTS','Java 21 LTS. Java 25 is the newer LTS, and the ideas here already live in 21','Whatever javac is on the instructor\'s laptop, with no target','JavaScript'],1,'21 is the current widely deployed LTS. 25 is newer. Pinning a target is how you know which syntax is legal.'),
F('Compile Hello.java from the terminal.','___ Hello.java',['javac','java','jar','jdb'],0,'javac is the compiler. java is the command that starts the JVM afterward.'),
M('Where does a normal program start?',['The first line of the file','The main method','The first field','A static import'],1,'The JVM looks for main and calls it. Other methods wait until something calls them.'),
O('System.out.println("ready");',['ready','READY','null','It does not compile'],0,'println writes the text and a newline. This is the body of main after the JVM has started.'),
B('This is supposed to launch the program. Tap the line that is the wrong command.',['javac Hello.java','java Hello.class'],1,'java wants the class name, Hello, not the file name Hello.class. javac is the step that consumes the .java file.'),
X('This compiles the wrong file. Which line compiles the source?','javac Hello.class',['javac Hello.java','javac Hello.class','java Hello.class','java --compile Hello'],0,'javac reads source and writes bytecode. Hello.class is the output, not the input.')
]};
const memory={id:'java.memory',name:'Memory: Values and References',
tip:'A variable is a named slot. A primitive stores the value in the slot, so assigning copies the value. An object lives on the heap, and the slot stores a reference: a way to find the object, not the object itself. Local slots live in the method\'s stack frame and disappear when the method returns. The heap object stays until nothing points at it. Two variables can hold the same reference. Assigning one of them to a new object does not change the other.',
sheet:['int y = x copies the number. Later changes to y do not touch x.','String b = a copies the reference. Both names find the same object until one is reassigned.','Locals live in the stack frame of the call. Objects live on the heap.','null is a reference to nothing. The slot exists; the object does not.','Reassigning a variable changes that slot only.'],
ex:[
O('int x = 1;\nint y = x;\ny = 2;\nSystem.out.println(x + " " + y);',['1 2','2 2','1 1','2 1'],0,'x and y each hold an int. y = x copies the value 1. y = 2 writes y\'s slot and leaves x alone.'),
O('String a = "cat";\nString b = a;\na = "dog";\nSystem.out.println(b);',['dog','cat','null','catdog'],1,'b = a copies the reference, so both point at "cat". a = "dog" points a somewhere else. b still finds "cat".'),
M('Where does a String object live?',['In the variable, the way an int does','On the heap. The variable holds a reference.','In the class file only','On the call stack, as the characters themselves'],1,'The slot is small and holds a reference. The characters live with the object on the heap.'),
M('What does null mean?',['The number 0','An empty String','The reference points at no object','The variable was never declared'],2,'The slot exists and is empty of an object. Calling a method through it has nothing to call.'),
F('Objects are allocated on the ___.','Objects are allocated on the ___.',['heap','stack','register','source file'],0,'The heap is the pile of objects. The stack is the chain of method calls and their local slots.'),
B('Tap the line that throws.',['String s = null;','int n = s.length();','System.out.println(n);'],1,'s points at nothing. length() needs an object. This is a NullPointerException, and the stack trace names this line.'),
M('You pass an int to a method and the method adds one to its parameter. The caller\'s variable…',['Becomes one larger, because Java passes objects','Stays the same, because the method got a copy of the value','Becomes null','Is moved to the heap'],1,'The parameter is a new slot. Filling it does not write the caller\'s slot. Objects are different only because the copied value is a reference.'),
X('Calling length on null throws. Which line is safe when s might be null?','String s = null;\nint n = s.length();',['int n = s == null ? 0 : s.length();','int n = s.length();','int n = s?.length();','int n = length;'],0,'Test the reference before you use it. Java has no ?. operator. The other variable still holds whatever it held.')
]};
const debug={id:'java.debug',name:'Debugging Mindset',
tip:'A failure is evidence, not a verdict on you. Read the exception type first: it is the program\'s hypothesis (null, wrong index, wrong type). Then read the stack trace from the top. The top frame is the line that threw. Frames under it are the callers, which is the call stack from the memory lesson. Make the smallest program that still fails, change one thing, and keep the change only if the evidence moved. Swallowing an exception deletes the evidence.',
sheet:['Name the exception before you edit code.','The top of the stack trace is the throw. Your method is often a frame below.','Reduce the input until the failure is obvious, then change one thing.','An empty catch hides the bug and lets the program limp on.','Print the value you believe, on the line before the line that uses it.'],
ex:[
M('A program throws. What is the first thing to read?',['A random line, and change it','The exception type and the top stack frame','The whole file, from the imports','Nothing. Run it again and hope.'],1,'The type says what went wrong. The top frame says where it was thrown. That is the evidence.'),
M('The stack trace lists main, then sort, then get, with get on top. What is true?',['main threw the exception','get threw, and sort called get, and main called sort','The bottom frame ran last','The trace is the source code in order'],1,'Each frame is a method call that has not returned. The throw is the top. The callers are underneath, which is the stack.'),
M('The failure happens only on a 10,000 line file. What do you do?',['Keep the whole file so the bug "stays realistic"','Cut the input down until a tiny case still fails','Add more features so the bug gets diluted','Catch Exception and continue'],1,'A small failing case is an experiment you can understand. The big file has too many suspects.'),
F('The exception for calling a method on null is ___.','The exception for calling a method on null is ___.',['NullPointerException','IOException','ClassCastException','StackOverflowError'],0,'The name is the hypothesis: a reference was null. The stack frame tells you which one.'),
B('This "handles" a failure by deleting it. Tap the line that hides the bug.',['try {','    parse(input);','} catch (Exception e) {','}'],3,'An empty catch means the failure happened and the program pretends it did not. At least log it, or do not catch it yet.'),
M('"It works when I run it" and it fails for someone else. The useful question is…',['Whose laptop is newer','What is different: input, directory, JDK version, or the line that changed','Whether Java is installed at all','Whether to rewrite the method in another language'],1,'A bug is a difference between what you believe and what ran. Find the difference.'),
X('The value is wrong and you cannot see it. Which line shows it before the crash line?','int n = compute();\nuse(n);',['System.out.println(n);\nuse(n);','use(n);','compute();','n = null;'],0,'Print the value you think you have, on the line before the line that depends on it. Guessing inside use is how the evidence gets muddy.')
]};
const modern={id:'java.modern',name:'Modern Java Syntax',
tip:'Java 21 can be shorter without becoming a different language. var infers a local type and then freezes it. A text block is still a String: the indent shared with the closing quotes is removed, and the line break stays. An arrow switch yields a value and does not fall through the way a classic switch does. instanceof String s tests the type and names the value, so the cast is not a second step. when adds a boolean guard to a pattern. None of these change the memory model.',
sheet:['var is inference, not a dynamic type.','A text block keeps the newline and strips the indent of the closing quotes.','Arrow switch yields a value. It does not fall through.','instanceof String s binds s only when the test passes.','case Integer i when i > 0 is a pattern plus a guard.'],
ex:[
M('After `var title = "Ada";`, which later line compiles?',['title = 3;','title = "Grace";','title = null is always illegal for a var','var title = title;'],1,'title was inferred as String. Assigning another String is fine. Assigning an int is a type error. null is a legal String reference, and it is a separate question.'),
O('String s = """\n    hello\n    """;\nSystem.out.println(s.equals("hello\\n"));',['true','false','hello','hello\\n'],0,'The closing quotes sit at the same indent as the text, so the indent is stripped. The newline at the end of the line stays, and "hello\\n" is that string. Run on JDK 21.'),
O('int day = 2;\nint n = switch (day) {\n    case 1 -> 10;\n    case 2 -> 20;\n    default -> 0;\n};\nSystem.out.println(n);',['10','20','0','2'],1,'An arrow switch picks one arm and yields it. day 2 selects 20. There is no fall-through into default.'),
O('Object o = "hi";\nSystem.out.println(o instanceof String s ? s.length() : 0);',['0','2','hi','true'],1,'The value is a String, so the pattern matches and s is that string. length is 2. If it were not a String, the result would be 0.'),
O('Object z = 3;\nSystem.out.println(switch (z) {\n    case Integer i when i > 0 -> "pos";\n    case Integer i -> "other";\n    default -> "no";\n});',['pos','other','no','3'],0,'3 is an Integer and it passes the guard i > 0, so the arm is "pos". A negative integer would be "other". A non-integer would be "no".'),
F('Bind the string only when the value really is a String.','if (o instanceof String ___) { use(s); }',['s','s when true','as String','-> s'],0,'The name comes right after the type. That name exists only inside the branch where the test passed.'),
B('This switch expression does not compile. Tap the line the compiler rejects.',['int day = 2;','int n = switch (day) {','    case 1 -> 10;','    case 2 -> 20;','};'],1,'An int has more values than 1 and 2. A switch expression has to cover every value, so this needs a default arm (or another way to be exhaustive). The arrow arms themselves are legal. javac reports the switch.'),
X('The classic switch falls through and prints too much. Which expression prints only Tue?','int day = 2;\nswitch (day) {\n    case 1: System.out.println("Mon");\n    case 2: System.out.println("Tue");\n    default: System.out.println("?");\n}',['System.out.println(switch (day) { case 1 -> "Mon"; case 2 -> "Tue"; default -> "?"; });','switch (day) { case 1: System.out.println("Mon"); case 2: System.out.println("Tue"); default: System.out.println("?"); }','System.out.println(day == 2);','case 2: System.out.println("Tue");'],0,'The arrow form selects one arm and yields its value. The colon form, as written, enters at Tue and continues into default.')
]};
const sealed={id:'java.sealed',name:'Sealed Types',
tip:'A sealed type lists who may extend or implement it. The set is closed: a new class in another file cannot join unless it is named in permits. The payoff is a switch that the compiler can prove is exhaustive, with no default arm to rot. Use an enum when the constants themselves are the data. Use a sealed interface when each case is its own type and carries different fields, which is what a record is good at. Records were the previous lessons; this is the closed set around them.',
sheet:['permits names the allowed subtypes. Nobody else can extend the type.','A switch on a sealed type can cover every case and skip default.','An enum is one type with fixed constants. A sealed hierarchy is several types.','The subtypes are final, sealed, or non-sealed. A record that implements the interface is final.','This is a Java 17 feature, and the pattern switch that uses it comfortably is Java 21.'],
ex:[
M('What does sealed buy you?',['Faster methods','The compiler knows the full set of subtypes','Private fields','Multiple inheritance of classes'],1,'A closed set means a switch can be checked. An open class hierarchy cannot make that promise.'),
M('Circle is not in permits. Which is true?',['Circle can still implement the interface if it is public','Circle does not compile as an implementation','Circle becomes an enum','permits is only a comment'],1,'permits is the allow-list. A type that is not on it is not a legal subtype.'),
O('sealed interface Shape permits Circle {}\nrecord Circle(int r) implements Shape {}\n// in main:\nShape s = new Circle(3);\nSystem.out.println(switch (s) {\n    case Circle c -> c.r();\n});',['3','Circle','0','null'],0,'s is a Circle, the only permitted type, so the switch binds c and reads r, which is 3. No default arm is required because the set is closed. Run on JDK 21.'),
F('Name Circle as the only implementation.','sealed interface Shape ___ Circle {}',['permits','extends','allows','of'],0,'permits introduces the allow-list. extends is what a class does, not what the sealed parent does here.'),
M('When is an enum the better model?',['Each case needs different fields','The values are a fixed set of constants with the same shape','You need a method that returns a new subtype','You want subclasses in other packages'],1,'PENNY and NICKEL are constants. A circle versus a labeled polygon, with different data, wants a sealed interface of records.'),
B('This switch is on a sealed Shape that permits only Circle, and it does not compile. Tap the useless arm.',['return switch (s) {','    case Circle c -> c.r();','    default -> 0;','};'],2,'The default can never run, and on a sealed type the compiler wants the real cases. Cover Circle and drop the default, or the arm hides a future permits change badly.'),
X('Square was added as a subtype without being invited. Which declaration allows only Circle?','interface Shape {}\nrecord Circle(int r) implements Shape {}\nrecord Square(int w) implements Shape {}',['sealed interface Shape permits Circle {}','interface Shape {}','class Shape permits Circle {}','enum Shape { Circle, Square }'],0,'Sealing the interface and permitting Circle makes Square illegal. A plain interface is open. An enum would erase the fields the records were holding.')
]};
const render={id:'css.render',name:'How the Browser Paints',
tip:'The browser does not show your file. It parses the HTML into a DOM, a tree of objects, one per element. It parses the CSS into the CSSOM, the rules with their selectors. It combines the two into a render tree of the boxes that are actually visible, lays those boxes out (this is where the box model in the next lesson gets its sizes), and paints pixels. Change a class and it may have to redo style, layout, and paint. That pipeline is why a selector is not free and why layout properties cost more than color.',
sheet:['HTML becomes the DOM. CSS becomes the CSSOM. Neither is the picture yet.','The render tree keeps the boxes that are visible. display: none leaves a node out of it.','Layout decides sizes and positions. Paint fills pixels.','The cascade resolves competing rules before layout runs.','A later lesson makes the box model precise. Here, the idea is that every element becomes a box.'],
ex:[
M('What is the DOM?',['The pixels on the screen','A tree of objects, one per element, built by parsing the HTML','The CSS file','The network cache'],1,'Parsing turns tags into objects you can walk. The picture comes later.'),
M('Which step decides the size and position of each box?',['Download','Layout','Gzip','The alt attribute'],1,'Layout is the geometry pass. Paint comes after the boxes have places.'),
M('An element with display: none…',['Is in the render tree, just transparent','Is in the DOM and left out of the render tree','Is removed from the HTML file','Becomes a comment'],1,'The node still exists for scripts. The render tree does not include a box for it, so layout and paint skip it.'),
F('CSS is parsed into the ___.','CSS is parsed into the ___.',['CSSOM','DOM','JPEG','JVM'],0,'The CSSOM is the rule tree. The DOM is the element tree. Layout reads both.'),
M('You change only a text color. Which cost can you usually avoid?',['Style calculation','Layout of every box','Paint of that text','All of them. Color is free.'],1,'Color does not change the size of the box, so layout can stay. Paint still runs. This is why the variables lesson prefers transform and opacity for animation.'),
B('Someone says the browser "draws the tags from top to bottom and then stops." Tap the step that story skips.',['Parse HTML into a DOM','Parse CSS','Combine and lay out boxes','Paint'],2,'A tag is not a drawing command. The browser has to decide boxes, and CSS changes those boxes, before any pixel is final.'),
X('The node should stay in the document but take no box. Which declaration matches that?','opacity: 0;',['display: none;','visibility: hidden;','opacity: 0;','color: transparent;'],0,'display: none removes the box from the render tree and leaves the node in the DOM. opacity: 0 and visibility: hidden keep the box. color does not change layout.')
]};
const cssModern={id:'css.modern',name:'Modern CSS',
tip:'A handful of CSS features are now the ordinary way to write a layout. Custom properties (--space) are values you can change in a smaller scope, which is how a theme works. Nesting puts a child rule inside the parent it belongs to, so the stylesheet reads like the tree. :has() styles an element because of what is inside it, which used to require a script. Container queries respond to the component\'s box instead of the whole viewport. Logical properties (margin-inline, padding-block) follow the writing direction, so "start" still means start in a right-to-left page.',
sheet:['var(--name) reads a custom property. The dashes are part of the name.','Nesting uses & for the parent selector.',':has(img) matches an element that contains an img.','A container query measures an ancestor you marked, not the window.','margin-inline is the two sides. margin-left is only the physical left.'],
ex:[
F('Read the custom property named space.','padding: var(___);',['--space','space','var-space','$space'],0,'The property was declared as --space. var() keeps those dashes.'),
M('Which selector styles a card only when it contains an image?',['card img','img > .card','.card:has(img)','.card::img'],2,':has(img) is a condition on the card, based on its descendants. The other selectors style the image or mean something else.'),
M('A card is in a narrow sidebar on a wide screen. Which query should change the card?',['@media (max-width: 400px), because the window is what matters','@container, after the sidebar is marked as a container','A script that reads window.innerWidth','print'],1,'The window is wide and the card is not. A container query measures the ancestor you name, which is the component\'s real room.'),
F('Set the start and end margins without saying left or right.','___: 1rem;',['margin-inline','margin-left','margin-physical','padding-left'],0,'margin-inline follows the writing direction. margin-left is always the physical left, which is wrong for a right-to-left page.'),
M('Inside `.card { & button { } }`, what does & mean?',['Every button on the page','The .card this rule is nested in','A class named and','The parent document'],1,'& is the nesting parent. The rule is .card button, written next to the rest of the card.'),
B('This was meant to style the card when it contains an error. Tap the selector that styles the error instead.',['.card:has(.error) { outline: 2px solid red; }','.card .error { outline: 2px solid red; }'],1,'A space is a descendant selector: it styles the error. :has styles the card that has the error.'),
X('This physical margin breaks in a right-to-left layout. Which line follows the writing direction?','margin-left: 1rem;',['margin-inline-start: 1rem;','margin-left: 1rem;','left: 1rem;','padding-left: 1rem;'],0,'inline-start is the start edge of the line. left is always left.'),
M('What do you set on the element that container queries should measure?',['container-type: inline-size','display: query','@media on that element','position: container'],0,'container-type makes the element a query container. Descendants then write @container rules against it.')
]};
const observe={id:'swift.observe',name:'Observable Models & Navigation',
tip:'SwiftUI draws a view from the state it reads, then draws it again when that state changes. @State is storage the view itself owns. A model that several screens share is a class, because a struct would be copied and the screens would stop seeing one object. Mark the class with the @Observable macro instead of the older ObservableObject and @Published pair. NavigationStack is the container for pushing screens. NavigationView is the old container; new code should not use it. A NavigationLink pushes a value, and navigationDestination decides which view that value shows.',
sheet:['@State is owned by one view. A shared model is a class.','@Observable is the current macro. ObservableObject is the previous protocol.','Assigning a class copies the reference, so two views can share the model.','NavigationStack replaces NavigationView.','NavigationLink(value:) pushes a value. navigationDestination maps that value to a view.'],
ex:[
M('Data that one view owns, such as whether a disclosure is open, belongs in…',['A global variable','@State on that view','ObservableObject in every file','@Published on the view struct'],1,'@State is view-owned storage. Changing it invalidates the body that read it.'),
M('Several screens must see the same account object. The model should be…',['A struct, so each screen gets a private copy','A class marked @Observable, so they share one object','An enum with no data','A protocol with no type'],1,'A class is a reference type. @Observable tells SwiftUI which properties the views read. A struct copy would split the account in two.'),
O('class Score {\n    var n = 0\n}\nlet a = Score()\nlet b = a\nb.n = 2\nprint(a.n)',['0','2','nil','Score'],1,'a and b are references to one Score. b.n = 2 writes that object, so a.n is 2. A struct would have copied and a.n would stay 0. This is why a shared SwiftUI model is a class.'),
F('Mark a shared model with the current macro.','___ class Account { var name = "" }',['@Observable','@Published','@StateObject','ObservableObject'],0,'@Observable is the macro. @Published was the per-property marker on an ObservableObject, which is the older style.'),
M('New code should push screens with…',['NavigationView','NavigationStack','UINavigationController in the view body','segue'],1,'NavigationStack is the current container. NavigationView is deprecated and behaves worse with the value-based links.'),
F('Push a value, then say which view it shows.','NavigationStack { NavigationLink(value: id) { Text(id) } .___(for: String.self) { id in Text(id) } }',['navigationDestination','navigationView','sheet','alert'],0,'navigationDestination(for:) maps the pushed value\'s type to the next view. The link only provides the value.'),
B('This is the old observation style. Tap the line that the @Observable macro replaces.',['class Account: ObservableObject {','    @Published var name = ""','}'],0,'Conforming to ObservableObject and marking each field @Published is the previous API. A class marked @Observable does both jobs.'),
X('This opens the old navigation container. Which line is the current one?','NavigationView { Text("Home") }',['NavigationStack { Text("Home") }','NavigationView { Text("Home") }','Navigation { Text("Home") }','StackNavigation { Text("Home") }'],0,'NavigationStack is the container to write now. NavigationView should not appear in new code.')
]};
const asyncL={id:'swift.async',name:'Async, Await, and the Main Actor',
tip:'A function marked async can pause. await marks the pause, and the thread is free for other work until the result arrives. The caller does not sit in a loop. That is the difference between waiting and blocking. Swift 6 checks sendable data more strictly so a value is not mutated from two tasks by accident. View state belongs on the main actor. After an await that ran work off the main actor, hop back before you write @State or an @Observable property a view is showing. Task { } starts asynchronous work from a button, which is a synchronous place.',
sheet:['async marks a function that can pause. await marks the pause.','await does not block the thread. The task resumes when the value is ready.','Task { } starts async work from synchronous code, such as a button action.','UI state is main-actor state. Update it on the main actor.','Swift 6\'s stricter checks exist to catch data races at compile time.'],
ex:[
M('What does await mean?',['Sleep this thread for a fixed time','Pause this task until the value is ready, and let the thread do other work','Run the next line on a background queue always','Cancel the task'],1,'The task is waiting. The thread is not stuck inside your function.'),
M('A button action is synchronous. How do you start an async load from it?',['Call the async function directly','Task { await load() }','async on the button','Thread.sleep'],1,'Task creates an asynchronous context. The button itself cannot be async.'),
O('func doubled(_ n: Int) async -> Int { n * 2 }\nlet t = Task { await doubled(21) }\nprint(await t.value)',['21','42','84','nil'],1,'doubled is async only so it is allowed to pause. It returns 42. Task starts it, and await t.value reads the result. Checked on Swift 6.0.3.'),
F('Mark a function that pauses for the network.','func load() ___ -> String { "" }',['async','await','throws','main'],0,'async is on the function. await is at the call. throws is a different kind of failure, from the errors lesson.'),
M('You awaited a download and then write straight into @State that a view is showing. What is the rule?',['@State is safe from any thread','Do that write on the main actor','@State can only be read, never written','The write must be inside a struct method marked mutating, on a background thread'],1,'The view reads that state on the main actor. Writing it from a random task is a data race, and Swift 6 is willing to reject it.'),
B('This calls an async function from a synchronous one and does not compile. Tap the line that is missing a task.',['func onTap() {','    let text = await load()','}'],1,'await is only legal in an async context. onTap is not async. Wrap the work in Task { await load() }.'),
X('The view updates from a background task. Which hop is the usual fix before writing state?','text = try await download()',['await MainActor.run { text = loaded }','text = try await download()','DispatchQueue.global().async { text = loaded }','Thread.sleep(1)'],0,'Download off the main actor, then assign the finished value on the main actor. Assigning text from a global queue is the race.')
]};

